**Heavy water** (D_{2}O, deuterium oxide) is about 11% denser than ordinary water. India's first heavy water plant came up at **Nangal** (Punjab, 1962) with the fertiliser plant there; the **Heavy Water Board** (Mumbai) now runs plants at Kota, Manuguru, Thal, Hazira, Tuticorin and Talcher, and India is one of the largest producers and an exporter.

@q 11:36

@q 11:30-31

@qnote 11:30 | The 500 MWe **Prototype Fast Breeder Reactor** at Kalpakkam is designed to use **mixed oxide (MOX)** fuel of plutonium and uranium, while the small **Fast Breeder Test Reactor** (1985) uses a **plutonium-uranium carbide** fuel. The source keys (b), statements 1 and 3.

A **breeder reactor** makes more fissile fuel than it burns by turning U-238 into Pu-239 (or Th-232 into U-233) with **fast neutrons**, so it needs no moderator and uses **liquid sodium** as coolant. Core loading of the PFBR at Kalpakkam began in **March 2024**.

@section 9.5 | India's Nuclear Map

@q 11:20-21

@table
India's three-stage programme (Homi Bhabha, 1954) | Reactor type | Fuel
Stage 1 | **Pressurised Heavy Water Reactors** (PHWR) | Natural uranium → produces plutonium
Stage 2 | **Fast Breeder Reactors** (PFBR, Kalpakkam) | Plutonium + uranium; breeds more fuel, starts thorium conversion
Stage 3 | Advanced thorium reactors (AHWR) | **Thorium** → U-233 (India has about a quarter of world thorium, in **monazite sands** of Kerala, Tamil Nadu, Odisha)
@end

**Research reactors** at **BARC, Trombay**: **Apsara** (1956, Asia's first), **CIRUS** (1960, closed 2010), **Dhruva** (1985), **Purnima**; and **Kamini** (Kalpakkam, uses U-233).

